Functions capabilities.

00:00:17.360 --> 00:00:17.990
I'm Vinod.

00:00:17.990 --> 00:00:21.020
I'm a product manager at Google.

00:00:21.020 --> 00:00:23.320
SARA FORD: Hi, I'm Sara,
and I'm a Cloud Developer

00:00:23.320 --> 00:00:24.520
Advocate at Google.

00:00:26.447 --> 00:00:28.530
VINOD RAMACHANDRAN: Just
the overview of our talk.

00:00:28.530 --> 00:00:30.322
First, we'll provide
you a product overview

00:00:30.322 --> 00:00:31.290
on Cloud Functions.

00:00:31.290 --> 00:00:33.300
Then, walk you through
key FaaS and function

00:00:33.300 --> 00:00:35.190
as a service use cases.

00:00:35.190 --> 00:00:38.130
Then, walk you through the
FaaS programming model.

00:00:38.130 --> 00:00:40.090
Then, we'll cover key
performance, scalability

00:00:40.090 --> 00:00:43.170
and availability features
in Cloud Functions.

00:00:43.170 --> 00:00:45.960
Then, walk you through
key new security features

00:00:45.960 --> 00:00:49.940
in Cloud Functions, as well
as cover the cloud event

00:00:49.940 --> 00:00:52.690
support that is being added
to Cloud Functions as well.

00:00:52.690 --> 00:00:55.750
And to conclude, we'll cover
a couple of exciting demos

00:00:55.750 --> 00:00:57.670
provided to you by Sara.

00:00:57.670 --> 00:01:00.320
So let's get started.

00:01:00.320 --> 00:01:02.150
Cloud Functions.

00:01:02.150 --> 00:01:04.580
Cloud Functions is Google
Cloud's event-driven serverless

00:01:04.580 --> 00:01:05.882
compute platform.

00:01:05.882 --> 00:01:08.090
Cloud Functions lets you
write small snippets of code

00:01:08.090 --> 00:01:11.300
that react to events
such as HTTP as well

00:01:11.300 --> 00:01:12.530
as asynchronous events.

00:01:12.530 --> 00:01:15.560
For example, uploading a file
to a cloud storage bucket

00:01:15.560 --> 00:01:17.660
and doing some
processing after that

00:01:17.660 --> 00:01:19.340
or sending a message
to [INAUDIBLE]

00:01:19.340 --> 00:01:23.210
and processing the
message accordingly.

00:01:23.210 --> 00:01:25.310
Cloud Functions are
truly serverless.

00:01:25.310 --> 00:01:27.050
They scale seamlessly.

00:01:27.050 --> 00:01:29.270
You don't have to manage
any of the infrastructure,

00:01:29.270 --> 00:01:31.780
and you pay for what you use.

00:01:31.780 --> 00:01:34.210
Further, functions of a
streamlined development model

00:01:34.210 --> 00:01:36.250
where you can get
started very quickly

00:01:36.250 --> 00:01:38.590
and have it running as well.

00:01:38.590 --> 00:01:41.230
In addition, functions are
integrated with different GCP

00:01:41.230 --> 00:01:44.430
products through
events as well as HTTP.

00:01:47.050 --> 00:01:49.300
Key FaaS use cases that
customers and users

00:01:49.300 --> 00:01:52.660
use Cloud Functions are
serverless webhooks.

00:01:52.660 --> 00:01:56.350
For example, doing
a GitHub push commit

00:01:56.350 --> 00:02:01.000
and then processing a function
and posting a message to Slack.

00:02:01.000 --> 00:02:03.370
Real-time data processing
where you can upload files

00:02:03.370 --> 00:02:07.990
to a Cloud Storage bucket and
process the data using MLA APIs

00:02:07.990 --> 00:02:10.419
and store the result back.

00:02:10.419 --> 00:02:12.460
IoT backends, where
temperature and humidity

00:02:12.460 --> 00:02:15.160
sensors from all over the world
upload temperature and humidity

00:02:15.160 --> 00:02:15.890
data.

00:02:15.890 --> 00:02:17.350
This process is by
Cloud Functions,

00:02:17.350 --> 00:02:20.500
and you take corrective
action accordingly.

00:02:20.500 --> 00:02:22.990
Intelligent applications
such as Twilio chat

00:02:22.990 --> 00:02:25.600
bots that upload messages
to Cloud Functions,

00:02:25.600 --> 00:02:29.200
which are processed by a
natural language API or BigQuery

00:02:29.200 --> 00:02:32.830
for trend analysis.

00:02:32.830 --> 00:02:35.020
Functions in the FaaS
programming model

00:02:35.020 --> 00:02:36.940
are very easily deployable.

00:02:36.940 --> 00:02:42.060
You can deploy a function
from the UI, from the CLI,

00:02:42.060 --> 00:02:47.650
through the IDE using functions
frameworks, as well as the API.

00:02:47.650 --> 00:02:51.010
Here's a simple example if
you have a snippet of code,

00:02:51.010 --> 00:02:52.780
where it's going to
be deployed using

00:02:52.780 --> 00:02:55.540
a single command ready to
serve traffic in minutes.

00:02:58.360 --> 00:03:01.080
You can deploy these functions
in your favorite programming

00:03:01.080 --> 00:03:01.830
language.

00:03:01.830 --> 00:03:04.380
We are super excited to
ship these languages to you.

00:03:04.380 --> 00:03:08.340
Think about Node, Python,
Go, Java, .NET, Ruby and PHP.

00:03:12.340 --> 00:03:13.750
Along with the
programming model,

00:03:13.750 --> 00:03:16.990
functions offer key performance,
scalability and availability

00:03:16.990 --> 00:03:20.280
capabilities.

00:03:20.280 --> 00:03:22.560
We are super excited to
ship the min instances

00:03:22.560 --> 00:03:24.360
capability in Cloud Functions.

00:03:24.360 --> 00:03:26.460
Cold starts is a real
problem in serverless

00:03:26.460 --> 00:03:28.860
where whenever your
function starts up

00:03:28.860 --> 00:03:30.570
or your serverless
service starts up,

00:03:30.570 --> 00:03:32.280
you have a startup text.

00:03:32.280 --> 00:03:35.170
Min instances lets you
mitigate this problem,

00:03:35.170 --> 00:03:36.790
which are pre-run instances.

00:03:36.790 --> 00:03:40.020
So for example, in this podcast
transcription application,

00:03:40.020 --> 00:03:42.300
you can have a set of
pre-run instances with each

00:03:42.300 --> 00:03:45.570
of your functions in order
to improve end-to-end latency

00:03:45.570 --> 00:03:47.670
of your serverless workflow.

00:03:47.670 --> 00:03:49.950
Here is a GitHub
tutorial as well.

00:03:49.950 --> 00:03:51.510
Please feel free
to check it out.

00:03:54.480 --> 00:03:56.010
Longer processing.

00:03:56.010 --> 00:03:58.225
We're super excited to
ship longer processing

00:03:58.225 --> 00:03:59.100
with Cloud Functions.

00:03:59.100 --> 00:04:01.560
Now you can process your
functions up to 60 minutes.

00:04:01.560 --> 00:04:03.210
This capability is in preview.

00:04:03.210 --> 00:04:05.820
This is 6x the execution time.

00:04:05.820 --> 00:04:07.950
Think about longer data
processing pipelines

00:04:07.950 --> 00:04:09.600
and longer ML pipelines.

00:04:12.252 --> 00:04:13.710
Along with longer
processing, we're

00:04:13.710 --> 00:04:16.140
also shipping larger instances
with Cloud Functions.

00:04:16.140 --> 00:04:19.800
Now you can have up to 16 GB
of memory, which is in preview,

00:04:19.800 --> 00:04:22.470
as well as the ability
to have multiple CPUs.

00:04:22.470 --> 00:04:25.260
So think about larger
in memory operations

00:04:25.260 --> 00:04:28.110
as well as like [INAUDIBLE]
parallel applications

00:04:28.110 --> 00:04:29.040
with your functions.

00:04:32.530 --> 00:04:35.390
They've also expanded the number
of regions that are available

00:04:35.390 --> 00:04:38.305
with Cloud Functions, going
to about 22 regions globally.

00:04:41.510 --> 00:04:44.000
Along with the other
capabilities we talked about,

00:04:44.000 --> 00:04:45.950
we're adding some new
security capabilities

00:04:45.950 --> 00:04:47.690
in Cloud Functions.

00:04:47.690 --> 00:04:49.910
We are super excited
to ship the integration

00:04:49.910 --> 00:04:52.310
with Secrets Manager for
your Cloud Functions.

00:04:52.310 --> 00:04:56.030
Now you can securely set
your environment variables

00:04:56.030 --> 00:04:59.570
and access them using Secret
Manager in your Cloud Functions

00:04:59.570 --> 00:05:02.690
in a programmatic fashion and
making the developer experience

00:05:02.690 --> 00:05:03.740
seamless as well.

00:05:06.640 --> 00:05:08.410
Along with secrets,
we are also shipping

00:05:08.410 --> 00:05:10.600
a capability called
Customer-Managed Encryption

00:05:10.600 --> 00:05:11.450
Keys.

00:05:11.450 --> 00:05:14.830
So users can provide
user-provided encryption keys,

00:05:14.830 --> 00:05:18.340
which can then be used by
GCP to do all the encryption

00:05:18.340 --> 00:05:20.620
throughout the function flow.

00:05:20.620 --> 00:05:22.690
This gives more
control to the users

00:05:22.690 --> 00:05:25.220
and create a level of
encryption as well.

00:05:25.220 --> 00:05:29.140
And if the key does not work,
Google cannot even access

00:05:29.140 --> 00:05:32.500
the data, and it's completely
in the control of the user.

00:05:35.660 --> 00:05:37.440
Shared VPC support.

00:05:37.440 --> 00:05:39.890
We're also super excited
to ship this new networking

00:05:39.890 --> 00:05:42.650
capability, which
lets your functions

00:05:42.650 --> 00:05:45.020
access resources
in a shared VPC.

00:05:45.020 --> 00:05:47.060
You can access Compute
Engine resources

00:05:47.060 --> 00:05:49.850
as well as on prem systems.

00:05:49.850 --> 00:05:53.370
Further, this is within a
secure VPC-SC perimeter,

00:05:53.370 --> 00:05:55.760
which is a logical
boundary that adds

00:05:55.760 --> 00:05:58.490
additional restrictions such
as only authorized users

00:05:58.490 --> 00:06:02.120
can deploy a Cloud Function
within the perimeter

00:06:02.120 --> 00:06:04.850
as well as only authorized users
within the perimeter can invoke

00:06:04.850 --> 00:06:06.020
a function.

00:06:06.020 --> 00:06:08.630
Further, the function can be
configured behind a Cloud Load

00:06:08.630 --> 00:06:10.520
Balancer for load
balancing as well.

00:06:14.188 --> 00:06:15.980
In addition to the
networking capabilities,

00:06:15.980 --> 00:06:18.397
we're also shipping a capability
called private pools that

00:06:18.397 --> 00:06:19.880
gives users more
control on their

00:06:19.880 --> 00:06:23.157
build time environment
for their Cloud Function.

00:06:23.157 --> 00:06:25.490
Users can have more control
and how their Cloud Function

00:06:25.490 --> 00:06:28.760
is built, thereby letting users
control the entire software

00:06:28.760 --> 00:06:32.540
supply chain of code, build,
and invocations per run.

00:06:36.748 --> 00:06:38.290
Along with the
security capabilities,

00:06:38.290 --> 00:06:41.020
we are adding cloud event
support through Cloud Functions

00:06:41.020 --> 00:06:42.610
as well.

00:06:42.610 --> 00:06:44.920
Cloud events are
industry standard events,

00:06:44.920 --> 00:06:48.550
which are open and portable,
making your architecture

00:06:48.550 --> 00:06:49.810
for the long term as well.

00:06:53.030 --> 00:06:55.670
We added cloud event
support for Cloud Functions

00:06:55.670 --> 00:06:57.890
by supporting cloud audit logs.

00:06:57.890 --> 00:07:02.570
This lets you invoke functions
from over 90 plus GCP products.

00:07:02.570 --> 00:07:06.465
For example, every time you
create a compute engine or a VM

00:07:06.465 --> 00:07:08.090
instance, you can
now invoke a function

00:07:08.090 --> 00:07:10.350
to do some processing
after the VM creation,

00:07:10.350 --> 00:07:11.900
such as labeling the VM.

00:07:11.900 --> 00:07:13.520
Every time a BigQuery
job finishes,

00:07:13.520 --> 00:07:16.620
you can do some processing
after the job is completed.

00:07:16.620 --> 00:07:19.250
The same applies for SQL,
Spanner, so on and so forth.

00:07:22.430 --> 00:07:25.210
Now, I would like to
hand over to Sara,

00:07:25.210 --> 00:07:27.640
who has a couple of interesting
and exciting demos for you

00:07:27.640 --> 00:07:29.620
on these capabilities.

00:07:29.620 --> 00:07:31.430
SARA FORD: Thanks, Vinod.

00:07:31.430 --> 00:07:33.230
In the first demo,
you'll see how

00:07:33.230 --> 00:07:36.110
you can use min
instances to keep

00:07:36.110 --> 00:07:38.420
a certain number of
your applications

00:07:38.420 --> 00:07:44.390
warm to improve performance
time and minimize cold starts.

00:07:44.390 --> 00:07:47.270
And the reason why you
might want to do this

00:07:47.270 --> 00:07:49.340
is if, for example,
you know you're

00:07:49.340 --> 00:07:52.400
going to be receiving
additional traffic soon.

00:07:52.400 --> 00:07:56.360
Perhaps it's due to like a Black
Friday, Cyber Monday event,

00:07:56.360 --> 00:07:58.610
or you know that
you just need to get

00:07:58.610 --> 00:08:00.620
the best possible latency time.

00:08:00.620 --> 00:08:04.760
So by keeping a number
of instances warm,

00:08:04.760 --> 00:08:09.710
you can help reduce cold
starts and get the best latency

00:08:09.710 --> 00:08:13.950
and the response time possible.

00:08:13.950 --> 00:08:18.790
So you'll see how that
works in this upcoming demo.

00:08:18.790 --> 00:08:22.390
In this first demo, I
have a Node.js function,

00:08:22.390 --> 00:08:25.930
and it's already deployed with
min instances set to zero,

00:08:25.930 --> 00:08:28.630
which is the default.
So let's take

00:08:28.630 --> 00:08:30.880
a look at this function code.

00:08:30.880 --> 00:08:33.500
Now, the entry
point is helloWorld.

00:08:33.500 --> 00:08:37.630
So that means that all the
code outside of the entry point

00:08:37.630 --> 00:08:39.260
is going to be in global scope.

00:08:39.260 --> 00:08:43.570
So in other words, here is the
event handler, the entry point

00:08:43.570 --> 00:08:44.570
for the function.

00:08:44.570 --> 00:08:46.960
So any code outside
of that function,

00:08:46.960 --> 00:08:50.920
like here, as you see,
is in global scope.

00:08:53.830 --> 00:08:59.830
So line three, where we
call the start up method,

00:08:59.830 --> 00:09:05.470
this is going to get executed
any time there is a cold start,

00:09:05.470 --> 00:09:08.290
like the instances warming up.

00:09:08.290 --> 00:09:11.550
So we'll log that
this is a cold start,

00:09:11.550 --> 00:09:16.500
and then you'll see me
simulate a long connection.

00:09:16.500 --> 00:09:17.940
This could be something to--

00:09:17.940 --> 00:09:20.560
a call to establishing a
connection with the database.

00:09:20.560 --> 00:09:24.780
This could be an API call
that takes a while to respond,

00:09:24.780 --> 00:09:28.530
but something that you want
to do once, cache that value

00:09:28.530 --> 00:09:33.340
and have it available for future
invocations of your function.

00:09:33.340 --> 00:09:37.710
And so once this time-out
to sleep comes back,

00:09:37.710 --> 00:09:40.590
then you'll see me
log, I'm ready now,

00:09:40.590 --> 00:09:42.420
and then return, ready, back.

00:09:45.450 --> 00:09:48.630
So here is a code that
is invoked every time--

00:09:48.630 --> 00:09:51.300
that is executed every time
the function is evoked.

00:09:51.300 --> 00:09:55.380
And I'm using the utility for
just pure debugging purposes

00:09:55.380 --> 00:09:58.290
just to illustrate
what is happening.

00:09:58.290 --> 00:10:05.860
And it's getting the current
status of the promise.

00:10:05.860 --> 00:10:07.500
And if it's still
a cold start, it

00:10:07.500 --> 00:10:12.800
reports that, it waits until
the 30 second time-out has

00:10:12.800 --> 00:10:14.420
completed, and then
it says, now I'm

00:10:14.420 --> 00:10:17.390
warmed up, using the warm emoji.

00:10:17.390 --> 00:10:19.250
Otherwise, if it's
already a warm instance,

00:10:19.250 --> 00:10:22.400
then it just reports back
that it's a warm instance.

00:10:22.400 --> 00:10:24.800
And, of course, it
needs to respond back

00:10:24.800 --> 00:10:29.770
to the request with
request completed.

00:10:29.770 --> 00:10:34.810
So let's invoke our function
and see this in action.

00:10:34.810 --> 00:10:38.320
So on the trigger,
we'll simply click here.

00:10:38.320 --> 00:10:39.750
And this is going to take a--

00:10:39.750 --> 00:10:40.920
because of the 30 seconds.

00:10:43.480 --> 00:10:47.470
So we can go to cloud logging.

00:10:47.470 --> 00:10:50.560
And as that is
spinning up, we'll

00:10:50.560 --> 00:10:59.010
see it report, as we saw in
the code, I am a cold start.

00:10:59.010 --> 00:11:01.020
That will be logged first.

00:11:04.320 --> 00:11:05.220
So there it is.

00:11:05.220 --> 00:11:07.600
I'm a cold start.

00:11:07.600 --> 00:11:09.190
I'm still a cold start.

00:11:09.190 --> 00:11:12.320
So if we go back here,
we'll see I'm a cold start.

00:11:12.320 --> 00:11:15.910
And since we invoked it
while it was still pending,

00:11:15.910 --> 00:11:16.993
that's where we see this.

00:11:16.993 --> 00:11:18.410
And then as soon
as it comes back,

00:11:18.410 --> 00:11:19.690
it will say I'm warmed up.

00:11:19.690 --> 00:11:21.310
We see that the
request completed,

00:11:21.310 --> 00:11:28.240
and we'll see in the logs
the function completed.

00:11:28.240 --> 00:11:38.430
So now I am warmed
up, which is great.

00:11:38.430 --> 00:11:40.500
But let's say we
know in advance we're

00:11:40.500 --> 00:11:42.750
going to get future
invocations, what can

00:11:42.750 --> 00:11:45.240
we do to-- you might
be thinking, what can

00:11:45.240 --> 00:11:47.700
I do to have this ready to go?

00:11:47.700 --> 00:11:51.300
Maybe this connection, database
connection already established,

00:11:51.300 --> 00:11:56.250
or this API call, have
that value already cached,

00:11:56.250 --> 00:11:57.547
ready to go when there is--

00:11:57.547 --> 00:11:59.130
when you can predict
that you're going

00:11:59.130 --> 00:12:00.600
to have future invocations.

00:12:00.600 --> 00:12:03.420
And that's where min
instances comes in.

00:12:06.000 --> 00:12:10.360
And now you'll see me
redeploy the function using

00:12:10.360 --> 00:12:11.620
min instances.

00:12:11.620 --> 00:12:14.330
So let's go to edit.

00:12:14.330 --> 00:12:20.120
And then, in these advanced
features, scroll down,

00:12:20.120 --> 00:12:21.940
you'll see min instances.

00:12:21.940 --> 00:12:24.970
And instead of using
0, let's give it 5.

00:12:28.940 --> 00:12:30.260
And deploy that.

00:12:39.260 --> 00:12:43.760
Now that this function has
been deployed with the new min

00:12:43.760 --> 00:12:49.250
instances, we can go here and
see in details now that we

00:12:49.250 --> 00:12:50.420
have five min instances.

00:12:50.420 --> 00:12:51.590
We can confirm that.

00:12:51.590 --> 00:12:58.440
If we go to the logs now
and rerun this query,

00:12:58.440 --> 00:13:00.300
you'll see the call
function's updated.

00:13:00.300 --> 00:13:04.860
And you might think that this
is off by one, but instead

00:13:04.860 --> 00:13:07.840
of five cold starts because,
remember, we're spinning up

00:13:07.840 --> 00:13:10.420
five instances, you
might be thinking, well,

00:13:10.420 --> 00:13:11.760
why am I seeing six?

00:13:11.760 --> 00:13:14.280
Well, this first one is
called a health check.

00:13:14.280 --> 00:13:18.210
This is just to make sure that
your call function builds.

00:13:18.210 --> 00:13:19.810
And as a part of--

00:13:19.810 --> 00:13:22.320
not being invoked,
but just builds.

00:13:22.320 --> 00:13:24.780
And since this is
in global scope,

00:13:24.780 --> 00:13:27.270
that's going to get executed.

00:13:27.270 --> 00:13:28.950
But this is just a
health check just

00:13:28.950 --> 00:13:32.610
to make sure that the coding--

00:13:32.610 --> 00:13:34.890
there wasn't like a compile
error, or build error,

00:13:34.890 --> 00:13:39.510
or I'm missing dependency
or the function, identity,

00:13:39.510 --> 00:13:42.960
lock permissions, that's
what this health check is.

00:13:42.960 --> 00:13:45.300
So that's why you
see six of these.

00:13:45.300 --> 00:13:47.760
And now, if we were to go--

00:13:47.760 --> 00:13:49.260
we'll go back to the function.

00:13:49.260 --> 00:13:52.290
Go back to trigger.

00:13:52.290 --> 00:13:55.020
And you see, now you noticed
that it hit instantly.

00:13:59.770 --> 00:14:02.446
And if we go to cloud run--

00:14:02.446 --> 00:14:04.210
I'm sorry, run query.

00:14:07.010 --> 00:14:12.890
We may see that all the
instances were warm,

00:14:12.890 --> 00:14:16.440
and you see I'm a warm instance.

00:14:16.440 --> 00:14:19.120
So as you just
saw, you might want

00:14:19.120 --> 00:14:21.430
to consider using
min instances to keep

00:14:21.430 --> 00:14:26.710
a certain number of your
Cloud Functions instances

00:14:26.710 --> 00:14:31.120
warm in case you're expecting
an increase in traffic

00:14:31.120 --> 00:14:34.970
or you just need to get the
best latency times possible.

00:14:34.970 --> 00:14:37.450
Now, for the second
demo, you're going

00:14:37.450 --> 00:14:41.170
to see how a Cloud Function
can listen for and respond

00:14:41.170 --> 00:14:44.590
to a cloud audit log event.

00:14:44.590 --> 00:14:46.660
For example, let's
say that a company

00:14:46.660 --> 00:14:50.950
has a policy where
their engineers need

00:14:50.950 --> 00:14:55.390
to put their user name
as a label on any VM

00:14:55.390 --> 00:14:57.200
that they create.

00:14:57.200 --> 00:15:03.730
Well, you can have a checklist
for their engineers to follow,

00:15:03.730 --> 00:15:07.660
but they might forget or
they might have a typo.

00:15:07.660 --> 00:15:12.040
And so one of the best ways
to reduce human error is to,

00:15:12.040 --> 00:15:14.210
quote, "automated out."

00:15:14.210 --> 00:15:17.230
So instead, you could
write a Cloud Function

00:15:17.230 --> 00:15:20.560
to listen to a cloud audit
log event for whenever

00:15:20.560 --> 00:15:23.230
a VM is created and have the
Cloud Function automatically

00:15:23.230 --> 00:15:27.600
label the VM with their
user name instead.

00:15:27.600 --> 00:15:29.280
In this second
demo, you'll see how

00:15:29.280 --> 00:15:32.010
you can use a Cloud Function
to listen for and respond

00:15:32.010 --> 00:15:34.230
to a cloud audit log event.

00:15:34.230 --> 00:15:37.890
In this scenario, you'll see
a Cloud Function automatically

00:15:37.890 --> 00:15:42.900
label a VM using the user name
of the person who created it.

00:15:42.900 --> 00:15:45.260
So first, let's go over
how to deploy a function

00:15:45.260 --> 00:15:48.050
to respond to a cloud
audit log event.

00:15:48.050 --> 00:15:51.920
Now, notice in this deployment
you'll specify the trigger

00:15:51.920 --> 00:15:53.520
event filters.

00:15:53.520 --> 00:15:55.895
So we have a type,
cloud audit log.

00:15:59.020 --> 00:16:00.670
Service name is compute.

00:16:00.670 --> 00:16:04.870
And insert instances when
a new instance is created.

00:16:08.030 --> 00:16:10.870
So let's deploy that.

00:16:10.870 --> 00:16:14.560
And while that deploys, let's
take a look at the code.

00:16:18.740 --> 00:16:22.060
So the first thing to
notice is that the signature

00:16:22.060 --> 00:16:26.440
for the function event
handlers is different

00:16:26.440 --> 00:16:28.090
and now it's cloudevent.

00:16:28.090 --> 00:16:32.080
And so we grab the data from
the cloudevent data payload.

00:16:32.080 --> 00:16:35.368
And just in case an event
has more than one audit log,

00:16:35.368 --> 00:16:36.910
we want to make sure
we're responding

00:16:36.910 --> 00:16:40.010
to the very last event.

00:16:40.010 --> 00:16:46.050
The resource names, they look
like in this particular case.

00:16:46.050 --> 00:16:48.410
And so we want to break it
up and grab the instance

00:16:48.410 --> 00:16:50.930
name and the user name.

00:16:50.930 --> 00:16:54.550
And we log appropriately.

00:16:54.550 --> 00:16:58.020
Now, if you're trying
to-- like in this case,

00:16:58.020 --> 00:17:03.240
set a label, the API,
make sure that you're

00:17:03.240 --> 00:17:05.910
setting the most recent label.

00:17:05.910 --> 00:17:12.300
And so you need to get the
instance label fingerprint,

00:17:12.300 --> 00:17:15.060
otherwise you're not going
to be able to set the label.

00:17:15.060 --> 00:17:20.020
And then we can
invoke the VM label.

00:17:20.020 --> 00:17:24.220
And then, finally,
we log the results.

00:17:24.220 --> 00:17:27.329
So let's look at the
setVMLabel, the more interesting

00:17:27.329 --> 00:17:29.510
of the two.

00:17:29.510 --> 00:17:31.330
So here's the API call.

00:17:31.330 --> 00:17:34.270
We set up instanceName,
zone, project.

00:17:34.270 --> 00:17:37.420
Resource here, I have the
label named as creator.

00:17:37.420 --> 00:17:39.730
And then you'll see my
user name for this account.

00:17:39.730 --> 00:17:44.590
And then we make the API call to
set the label for the instance.

00:17:44.590 --> 00:17:47.090
Now, let's take a peek.

00:17:47.090 --> 00:17:51.170
And yes, our cloud function
has finished deploying.

00:17:51.170 --> 00:17:53.870
So let's jump over
to Cloud Console,

00:17:53.870 --> 00:18:01.320
and every refresh we'll see
the vmlabelerdemo is up.

00:18:01.320 --> 00:18:03.600
So let's go to the VMs
and create a new VM.

00:18:13.910 --> 00:18:15.340
And we'll just use the defaults.

00:18:23.420 --> 00:18:28.570
And as soon as that is created,
you'll see the label is added.

00:18:28.570 --> 00:18:32.920
And the nice thing about having
a Cloud Function automatically

00:18:32.920 --> 00:18:34.660
perform this task
is one less thing

00:18:34.660 --> 00:18:37.330
that you have to ask your
engineers or your developers

00:18:37.330 --> 00:18:39.760
or whoever is doing
the particular task.

00:18:39.760 --> 00:18:42.010
It's one less thing for
them to remember to do.

00:18:42.010 --> 00:18:46.900
So instead of having a long
checklist of, did I do this?

00:18:46.900 --> 00:18:47.690
Did I do that?

00:18:47.690 --> 00:18:50.470
The more you can automate
that out, the less human error

00:18:50.470 --> 00:18:51.830
you will have.

00:18:51.830 --> 00:18:54.970
So let's go to the instance.

00:18:54.970 --> 00:18:56.920
And now you can see
that the creator

00:18:56.920 --> 00:19:00.820
is drofaras, which
is my user name,

00:19:00.820 --> 00:19:04.750
or my full name, Sara Ford
spelled backwards for my demo

00:19:04.750 --> 00:19:06.100
account purposes.

